add(16,diagram([('Retain','√(1−βt) x(t−1)',B),('Independent','ηt ~ N(0,I)',A),('Add','√βt ηt',T)]),[eq(r'\sqrt{1-\beta_t}\,x_{t-1}'),eq(r'\eta_t\sim\mathcal N(0,I)'),eq(r'x_t=\sqrt{1-\beta_t}x_{t-1}+\sqrt{\beta_t}\eta_t')], 'Beta contributes variance; square root beta scales a standard Gaussian. Eta is independent per-step noise. We reserve epsilon for the aggregate noise in a marginal sample.', 'What is chosen and what is random?','The schedule is chosen; eta is random. Given the previous state, the next state is sampled.',layout='wide')
add(17,diagram([('α1 = 0.8','one-step retention',B),('α2 = 0.8','one-step retention',B),('ᾱ2 = 0.64','cumulative retention',T)]),[eq(r'\alpha_t=1-\beta_t'),eq(r'\bar\alpha_2=\alpha_1\alpha_2=0.64'),eq(r'\bar\alpha_t=\prod_{s=1}^{t}\alpha_s,\quad\bar\alpha_0=1')], 'Keep the bar visible. One alpha describes one transition; their product describes cumulative signal variance retention. The two-step schedule is deliberately large for arithmetic.')
add(18,'', [eq(r'x_2=\sqrt{\alpha_2\alpha_1}x_0+\sqrt{\alpha_2\beta_1}\eta_1+\sqrt{\beta_2}\eta_2'),eq(r'\alpha_2\beta_1+\beta_2=\alpha_2(1-\alpha_1)+(1-\alpha_2)=1-\alpha_1\alpha_2'),eq(r'q(x_t\mid x_0)=\mathcal N(\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}x_0,(1-\bar\alpha_t)I)'),eq(r'x_t=\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}x_0+\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\epsilon,\quad\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,I)')], 'Substitute the first step into the second. Independence allows the two variances to add. The last line samples the correct marginal; sharing epsilon across times does not reproduce the joint Markov chain.', 'Why do variances add?','The Gaussian noise terms are independent, so covariance cross terms vanish.',layout='wide')
add(19,img('digit.png','One coordinate; two-step arithmetic schedule'),[eq(r'x_0=1,\quad\bar\alpha_2=.64,\quad\epsilon=-.5'),eq(r'\sqrt{.64}=.8,\quad\sqrt{1-.64}=.6'),eq(r'.8(1)+.6(-.5)=.8-.3'),eq(r'x_2=.5')], 'The arithmetic schedule beta1=beta2=.2 leaves 64% signal variance retention, so its endpoint is not noise-only. Do not reuse it to justify a standard Gaussian sampling start.')
add(20,demo('noise'),[p('With fixed ε, the slider shares one noise array across times.'),p('In recursive mode, every step draws fresh noise.'),p('SNR = ᾱt / (1−ᾱt); at t=0 it is infinite.')], 'Move the timestep and select a coordinate. The slider computes real arrays and coefficients. Reset restores seed 667 and inputs; resample changes the random array explicitly. The linear training endpoint is approximately 4.04e-5.',layout='wide')
