add(29,diagram([('Dataset','representative examples?',B),('Corruption','makes target noise',A),('Learner','depends on data support',T)]),[p('We create the noise labels when we corrupt the images.'),p('We still need clean examples that represent the data we want to generate.')], 'A dataset containing only sevens can teach a seven-shaped prior but does not establish coverage of all digits. Self-generated noise labels remove one annotation need, not the need for representative data.')
add(30,diagram([('Prior term','q(xT|x0) versus p(xT)',A),('Local KLs','reverse matching',T),('Likelihood','reconstruct x0',B)]),[eq(r'\log p_\theta(x_0)\geq\operatorname{ELBO}(x_0)'),eq(r'q(x_{1:T}\mid x_0)=\prod_t q(x_t\mid x_{t-1})'),eq(r'p_\theta(x_{0:T})=p(x_T)\prod_t p_\theta(x_{t-1}\mid x_t)'),p('Negative bound = prior KL + local KL terms − expected log likelihood.')], 'Replace the VAE latent z by a whole latent path. q(x(t-1)|xt,x0) is tractable because training knows x0; q(x(t-1)|xt) integrates it out. The full equality, expectations and endpoint likelihood are in Appendices A2 and A3. The minus sign on log likelihood matters.',layout='wide')
add(31,'',[eq(r'\mathrm{KL}(\mathcal N(\tilde\mu_t,\tilde\beta_t I)\Vert\mathcal N(\mu_\theta,\sigma_t^2 I))=\frac{\|\tilde\mu_t-\mu_\theta\|^2}{2\sigma_t^2}+C'),eq(r'w_t=\frac{\beta_t^2}{2\sigma_t^2\alpha_t(1-\bar\alpha_t)},\quad t>1'),eq(r'\mathcal L_{simple}=\mathbb E_{x_0,t,\epsilon}\|\epsilon-\epsilon_\theta(x_t,t)\|^2')+p('Simple MSE drops the time-dependent weights and the other bound terms.')], 'C is independent of the learned mean for fixed covariances. Substituting the epsilon mean gives the shown weight. Never evaluate that t>1 weight at t=1 where posterior variance is zero. Endpoints and likelihood discretisation are also not retained by simplified MSE.',layout='wide')
add(32,img('fresh-gaussian.png','Fresh Gaussian starting state is sampled independently'),[eq(r'x_T\sim\mathcal N(0,I)'),p('Keep θ fixed and repeatedly sample a previous state.')], 'The displayed image illustrates a noisy state. Actual model sampling uses a new Gaussian draw with no clean reference. Intermediate random draws and the initial state are separate sources of stochasticity.')
add(33,'',[eq(r'x_t-\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\hat\epsilon'),eq(r'\hat x_0=\frac{x_t-\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\hat\epsilon}{\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}}'),eq(r'\hat x_0=\frac{.5-.6(-.4)}{.8}=.925')], 'First subtract estimated aggregate noise, then undo signal scaling. At large t, division by a very small coefficient amplifies prediction error. This is an estimated clean image; clipping a display does not validate it.',layout='wide')
add(34,diagram([('xt = .5','current noisy state',B),('x̂0 = .925','clean estimate',T),('x(t−1)','next noisy state',A)]),[p('The clean estimate asks what the original image might have been.'),p('The next state still needs the noise level associated with t−1.'),p('Pass the sampled x(t−1) to the next denoiser call.')], 'Keep the three quantities in separate labelled panels. Estimating clean data is useful for parameterising a mean, but cannot silently replace the next sampled chain state.', 'Which value enters the next call?','The sampled x(t-1).')
add(35,'',[eq(r'p_\theta(x_{t-1}\mid x_t)=\mathcal N(\mu_\theta(x_t,t),\sigma_t^2 I)'),eq(r'\hat\epsilon=\epsilon_\theta(x_t,t),\quad c_t=\frac{\beta_t}{\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}}'),eq(r'x_t-c_t\hat\epsilon'),eq(r'\mu_\theta=\frac{x_t-c_t\hat\epsilon}{\sqrt{\alpha_t}}')], 'This denominator is one-step alpha, not alpha-bar. The Gaussian parameterisation approximates the unknown reverse conditional; a coarse true reverse need not be Gaussian. Appendix A4 derives the tractable posterior and then substitutes the clean estimate.',layout='wide')
add(36,diagram([('Mean μθ','centre of transition',T),('Fresh ξ','uncertainty remains',A),('Previous state','μθ + σt ξ',B)]),[eq(r'\sigma_t^2=\tilde\beta_t=\beta_t\frac{1-\bar\alpha_{t-1}}{1-\bar\alpha_t}'),eq(r'x_{t-1}=\mu_\theta+\mathbf 1[t>1]\sqrt{\tilde\beta_t}\xi'),p('At t=1, return the mean without adding fresh noise.')], 'Posterior variance is this lecture choice; beta and learned variance are other historical options. A denoising transition need not improve each coordinate monotonically. Standard deviation is square root variance.',layout='wide')
add(37,demo('reverse'),[p('At reset: mean 0.708088; clean estimate 0.925.'),p('Variance 1/9; standard deviation 1/3.'),p('At reset, the fresh noise draw is z = 0.3.'),p('At reset, the sampled previous state is 0.808088.')], 'Keep every schedule value derived from beta=.2. The editable predicted noise and fresh noise drive the actual arithmetic. The final-step mode ignores fresh noise explicitly. The drawing is a Gaussian density, not a confidence interval.',layout='wide')
add(38,code('x = torch.randn(shape)\nfor step in range(T, 0, -1):\n    t = torch.full((B,), step, dtype=torch.long)\n    eps_hat = model(x, t)\n    mean = (x - beta[step] * eps_hat\n            / (1-alpha_bar[step]).sqrt())\n    mean = mean / alpha[step].sqrt()\n    if step > 1:\n        x = mean + posterior_var[step].sqrt() * randn()\n    else:\n        x = mean'),[p('Reuse the trained denoiser as time decreases from T to 1.'),p('Skip fresh noise at the last step and return the generated x0.')], 'The companion implements this exact loop with eval-mode restoration. Arbitrarily skipping steps changes the recurrence and is not a valid fast sampler. DDIM and respacing require separately defined transitions.')
